{
 "category": "44-environment-energy-climate",
 "cards": [
  {
   "id": 367,
   "method": "Environmentally-extended input-output footprints and consumption accounting",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/eeio_footprints.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_footprint",
    "env_multipliers",
    "env_embodied_trade"
   ],
   "when": "attributing emissions, materials or water to final consumption rather than to production, across the whole global supply chain",
   "when_not": "you need facility-level emissions; the multi-regional table is too aggregated for the sector in question",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "production-based accounting",
     "criterion": "the question is territorial emissions"
    },
    {
     "alt": "consumption-based accounting",
     "criterion": "the question is the footprint of demand"
    },
    {
     "alt": "life-cycle assessment",
     "criterion": "you need product-level rather than sector-level detail"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "footprint: the extension attributed to each final-demand category",
    "multipliers: extension per unit of final demand by sector",
    "trade_balance: emissions embodied in imports and exports",
    "sector_contributions: which sectors carry the footprint"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "consumption- and production-based accounts differ by the emissions embodied in TRADE, and for open economies that difference is large -- stating which is reported is not optional",
    "sector aggregation drives the answer; a coarse table attributes a clean product the average intensity of its sector",
    "the Leontief inverse assumes fixed technical coefficients, so it is a snapshot and not a projection"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Miller & Blair 2009, Input-Output Analysis: Foundations and Extensions, 2nd ed.",
    "Wiedmann 2009 (Ecological Economics 69:211-222)",
    "pymrio"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 368,
   "method": "Index decomposition analysis: Laspeyres, Paasche and log-mean Divisia",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/index_decomposition.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_decompose_index"
   ],
   "when": "attributing a change in emissions or energy use to activity, structure and intensity, so the drivers are separated from the total",
   "when_not": "the underlying data are inconsistent between periods; you need a supply-chain rather than an accounting decomposition (-> structural decomposition)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "LMDI",
     "criterion": "the standard: perfect decomposition with no residual"
    },
    {
     "alt": "Laspeyres",
     "criterion": "simple and familiar, but leaves a residual"
    },
    {
     "alt": "structural decomposition",
     "criterion": "you have input-output tables and want supply-chain detail"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "contributions: the change attributed to each factor",
    "residual: the unexplained part, zero by construction for LMDI",
    "cumulative: contributions accumulated over the period",
    "method: which index form produced the answer"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "Laspeyres and Paasche leave a RESIDUAL that can be as large as the effects; LMDI exists precisely because it does not",
    "LMDI needs a convention for zero and negative values, and the convention changes the answer materially",
    "additive and multiplicative forms answer different questions and their contributions are not interchangeable"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Ang 2005 (Energy Policy 33:867-871)",
    "Ang & Zhang 2000 (Energy 25:1149)"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 369,
   "method": "Integrated-assessment scenario handling, harmonisation and aggregation",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/iam_scenarios.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_scenario_filter",
    "env_scenario_harmonise",
    "env_scenario_aggregate"
   ],
   "when": "working with the scenario ensembles climate policy analysis rests on: filtering, harmonising to a common base year and aggregating regions",
   "when_not": "you have a single scenario and no ensemble to manage; the variables do not follow the standard template",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "scenario filtering and aggregation",
     "criterion": "you need to subset and summarise an ensemble"
    },
    {
     "alt": "harmonisation",
     "criterion": "scenarios start from different historical bases"
    },
    {
     "alt": "direct interpolation",
     "criterion": "you only need to fill a few missing years"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "data: the filtered and harmonised scenario set",
    "aggregates: regional or variable aggregations",
    "harmonisation_offsets: the adjustment applied to each scenario",
    "coverage: which scenarios and variables survived the filter"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "none",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "scenario ensembles are NOT probability distributions; a quantile across scenarios is a spread of modelling choices, not a confidence interval",
    "harmonisation to a common base year changes the near-term path of every scenario and must be reported",
    "region and variable definitions differ between models; aggregating without mapping them produces silent double counting"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Riahi et al. 2017 (Global Environmental Change 42:153-168)",
    "Huppmann, Gidden, Nicholls et al. 2021 (Open Research Europe 1:74)",
    "pyam-iamc"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 370,
   "method": "Environmental Kuznets curve and emissions-income panels",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/environmental_kuznets.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_ekc"
   ],
   "when": "testing whether emissions rise and then fall with income, in a panel where both country and period effects matter",
   "when_not": "the panel is too short to separate the quadratic from a trend; the turning point lies outside the observed income range",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "linear emissions-income elasticity",
     "criterion": "no turning point is claimed"
    },
    {
     "alt": "quadratic EKC",
     "criterion": "the inverted-U is the hypothesis"
    },
    {
     "alt": "semiparametric EKC",
     "criterion": "you do not want to impose the quadratic shape"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: income and income-squared coefficients",
    "turning_point: the income level at the peak, with its confidence interval",
    "in_sample: whether the turning point lies inside the observed income range",
    "fixed_effects: the effects absorbed"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "a turning point OUTSIDE the observed income range is an extrapolation, not a finding, and it is the single most common overclaim in this literature",
    "the quadratic imposes a symmetric inverted U; a semiparametric fit frequently shows something else entirely",
    "the delta-method interval on the turning point is wide and often includes implausible values -- report it"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Grossman & Krueger 1995 (Quarterly Journal of Economics 110:353-377)",
    "Stern 2004 (World Development 32:1419)",
    "Wagner 2008 (Resource and Energy Economics 30:388)",
    "pyfixest"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 371,
   "method": "Weather normalisation of energy demand with heating and cooling degree days",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/weather_normalisation.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_degree_days",
    "env_weather_normalise"
   ],
   "when": "separating the weather-driven part of energy demand from the underlying trend, so consumption can be compared across years",
   "when_not": "the weather data does not match the consumption region; the demand is not weather sensitive",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "degree-day regression",
     "criterion": "the standard, transparent approach"
    },
    {
     "alt": "changepoint regression",
     "criterion": "the base temperature must be estimated rather than assumed"
    },
    {
     "alt": "nonparametric temperature response",
     "criterion": "the response is not piecewise linear"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "normalised: weather-normalised demand",
    "hdd, cdd: the constructed degree-day series",
    "base_temperature: the balance point used or estimated",
    "weather_contribution: the demand attributed to weather"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the BASE TEMPERATURE is not universal -- it differs by building stock and climate, and assuming the conventional value can misattribute a large share of demand",
    "degree days computed from daily means differ from those computed from hourly data, sometimes materially",
    "normalisation removes weather, not behaviour; a mild year with a price shock is not disentangled by this alone"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Quayle & Diaz 1980 (Journal of Applied Meteorology 19:241-246)",
    "Hor, Watson & Majithia 2005 (IEEE Transactions on Power Systems 20:2078)",
    "statsmodels"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 372,
   "method": "Mapping climate scenarios onto macroeconomic paths: transition and physical risk",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/climate_macro_mapping.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_climate_macro_map",
    "env_damage_function"
   ],
   "when": "turning a climate scenario into the macro-financial variables a stress test or projection needs",
   "when_not": "the scenario has no macro variables and no mapping is available; the horizon is far beyond any credible macro relationship",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "scenario variable mapping",
     "criterion": "the scenario already carries macro variables"
    },
    {
     "alt": "elasticity-based translation",
     "criterion": "you must translate physical variables into macro ones"
    },
    {
     "alt": "damage functions",
     "criterion": "you want output loss as a function of warming"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "macro_paths: the mapped macroeconomic variables",
    "transition_component: the part driven by policy and carbon prices",
    "physical_component: the part driven by warming and hazards",
    "assumptions: the elasticities and damage function used"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "multi-line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "transition and physical risk have OPPOSITE time profiles -- strong early transition lowers late physical damage -- so reporting one without the other is systematically misleading",
    "damage functions differ by more than an order of magnitude across the literature; the choice dominates any long-horizon result",
    "these mappings are calibrated, not estimated; treat the output as a scenario, never as a forecast"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "NGFS 2023, Scenarios for central banks and supervisors",
    "Nordhaus 2017 (Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 114:1518-1523)",
    "Batten, Sowerbutts & Tanaka 2016, Let's talk about the weather (Bank of England Staff Working Paper 603)"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 373,
   "method": "Kaya identity and energy-balance accounting",
   "wrapper_file": "c44_environment_energy_climate/kaya_identity.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "env_kaya",
    "env_energy_balance"
   ],
   "when": "decomposing emissions into population, output per person, energy intensity and carbon intensity -- the standard framing for emissions drivers",
   "when_not": "the components are not available on a consistent basis; you need supply-chain detail (-> input-output)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "Kaya identity",
     "criterion": "the standard four-factor decomposition"
    },
    {
     "alt": "LMDI decomposition",
     "criterion": "you want a residual-free attribution of the change"
    },
    {
     "alt": "energy balance accounting",
     "criterion": "the question is energy flows rather than emissions"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "factors: the four Kaya components per period",
    "contributions: the change in emissions attributed to each",
    "identity_check: reconstruction error, which should be numerically zero",
    "energy_balance: supply, transformation and final consumption where requested"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the identity is an ACCOUNTING TAUTOLOGY, not a causal model -- a factor's contribution is not the effect of changing it, because the factors are not independent",
    "a non-zero identity check means the four series are on inconsistent bases or units",
    "carbon intensity of energy and energy intensity of output move together through fuel switching, so their separate contributions are not separable policies"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Kaya 1990, Impact of carbon dioxide emission control on GNP growth (IPCC Response Strategies Working Group)",
    "Raupach et al. 2007 (Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 104:10288)"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  }
 ],
